## Native macOS and Linux installers

The desktop release now treats macOS and Ubuntu/Debian as installed applications rather than raw executable downloads.

### macOS

- Intel x64, Apple Silicon ARM64, and Universal DMGs contain a real `Markdown Viewer.app`, application icon, Finder metadata, license files, installation instructions, and an Applications shortcut.
- Intel builds target the current native dependency floor of macOS 10.15; Apple Silicon builds target macOS 11. These are build baselines, not a claim of complete testing on every older system. CI launches installed packages on Intel and Apple Silicon macOS 15 and 26.
- Every Mach-O slice is checked for its advertised CPU, minimum OS, signature structure, and accidental ScreenCaptureKit dependency. The pinned Neutralino runtime is rebuilt instead of relabelling a newer binary.
- Tag releases can use Developer ID signing, hardened runtime, notarization, and stapling when repository credentials are configured. `MACOS-SIGNING.txt` records the actual state of this release; ad-hoc builds require the per-app Open Anyway flow and should never require disabling Gatekeeper globally.

### Ubuntu and Debian

- Recommended `.deb` packages are available for amd64, ARM64, and ARMHF. They install executable permissions, WebKitGTK/GTK/tray dependencies, a searchable desktop launcher, the Markdown Viewer icon, and AppStream metadata.
- Optional portable `.tar.gz` archives retain the same three CPU choices for users who manage their own system libraries.
- Package metadata identifies **Markdown Viewer**, publisher **ThisIs-Developer**, the **Apache-2.0** project license, maintainer, website, support link, description, and installed size. Ubuntu App Center can still show generic identity fields for a downloaded local DEB because that screen does not consume all packaged AppStream metadata.

### Stable application identity and data

All desktop packages keep application id `com.markdownviewer.desktop`, the existing restricted native API allowlist, and the fixed `Documents/Markdown Viewer Vault`. Replacing or uninstalling the application does not delete the vault. The release page documents platform trust prompts, fleet-deployment limits, checksums, and which packages are recommended for managed computers.

## Native file and Explorer workflows

The standard web and desktop application can import complete folder drops with nested and empty folders, precise Explorer destinations, bounded reads, progress feedback, and Private or Secret Workspace safeguards. Explorer now supports inline file/folder rename, F2, collision and case-only handling, multi-file ZIP download, and folder ZIP download with current drafts and safe duplicate names.

Desktop linked-file rename updates the original path while preserving its Markdown extension, refuses replacement of an occupied destination, and retains recoverable drafts when a filesystem operation is interrupted. Platform actions use **Open in File Explorer**, **Open in Finder**, or **Open in File Manager**.

The Windows single-instance, native shell-drop, and optional file-association work remains an explicitly isolated trial build. It uses separate identity and storage, does not set Windows UserChoice defaults, and does not broaden the standard desktop app's command permissions.

## Rich clipboard fidelity

Copying from Preview now captures the live computed appearance before sanitizing a detached selection. Supported receiving applications can retain inherited typography, theme backgrounds, individual borders, code wrappers and syntax tokens, semantic emphasis and links, and exact ordered or reversed list numbering.

The context menu records the selection when it opens and serializes only after **Copy** is chosen, so opening the menu stays responsive and stale selections are rejected. UI controls remain excluded, URLs are sanitized, image-only selections work, and every rich copy includes a plain-text alternative. Receiving applications can still rewrite or discard HTML and CSS.

## Preview position that survives transitions

Scroll state now belongs only to the visible, committed document and is not overwritten by a temporary loading skeleton or hidden pane. Restoration is guarded by document and render identity, retries while images or advanced renderers temporarily clamp the page, and yields immediately to user scrolling or navigation.

Editor coordinates restore before a view switch persists state, and Editor/Preview synchronization runs only while both panes are visible. The same rules apply to Live Share snapshots and the prepared desktop bundle.

## Clearer, complete PDF exports

The export dialog now names the two workflows by their result:

- **Standard PDF (Recommended):** fast browser/operating-system PDF generation with selectable, searchable text and configurable print settings.
- **Rendered PDF:** consistent image-based A4 pages for complex visual layouts.

Pagination tracks fractional text boundaries, overlapping ranges, tall cells, code, quotes, lists, repeated table headers, mixed font sizes, images, light and dark appearances, and cancellation or retry. Page slicing preserves source coverage instead of silently dropping lines near a boundary. Browser print settings can still add headers/footers or override page styling.

## Quality, localization, and distribution

All 15 locale catalogs retain matching keys and placeholders for native file operations, Explorer actions, PDF export names, and the v3.10.6 release-note labels. Root sources and prepared desktop resources remain synchronized.

Coverage now includes complete release manifests and tamper checks, Debian extraction and installed metadata, macOS architecture and load-command validation, native package launch/import/reopen checks, browser folder drops, linked rename recovery, Secret Workspace races, inline Explorer rename, folder and selection downloads, clipboard fidelity, Preview scroll lifecycle, and PDF pagination.

